WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:02.880
Your Netflix is Special

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.600
When you go to netflix.com or pull up the
netflix app on your phone, you’re not looking

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.559
at the same Netflix as everyone else.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:12.700
You’re looking at a special version of Netflix,
just for you.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.070
Almost everyone watching this will already
know that Netflix customizes what shows and

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.240
movies it displays based on your viewing habits.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.599
Netflix has said that they know if they don’t
get most people watching something within

00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:26.029
90 seconds, they’ll loose interest and move
on to something else.

00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:30.560
So when you look at this page you’re looking
at a complex system of multiple AI’s working

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.890
together to help you find something you want
to watch as quickly as possible.If you’re

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.522
like me you might have noticed recently that
there are multiple versions of the artwork

00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:40.230
for a lot of show and movies.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.300
I found this interesting, but didn’t think
much of it, thinking that possibly they just

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.070
rotated through various images to make the
library of available shows and movies looking

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.080
fresh and interesting.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.690
The Netflix streaming homepage used to look
something like this.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:58.480
Artwork for videos was adapted from or very
similar too the poster or dvd cover artwork.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.060
But what’s designed to grab your attention
on a large poster or dvd case, isn’t usually

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:07.830
the best for a small thumbnail on a website,
Netflix realized this has been making custom

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.580
artwork for shows and movies for a couple
years now.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.520
But it’s not enough to just customize things
for the site.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.530
In December 2017 Netflix released an article
detailing the way they use machine learning

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.530
to personalize the images displayed for each
user.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:26.300
The idea is simple, show users the image that
will show them what aspect of a piece of media

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.580
they’ll be most interested in.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.910
Here’s one of the examples they use, do
you watch a lot of Uma Thurman movies?

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.430
Then you’ll probably get this artwork for
Pulp Fiction.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.479
Never watch anything with Uma in it, but have
watch everything with John Travolta that netflix

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:40.479
has to offer?

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:42.670
You’ll probably get this version of the
artwork.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.680
It can also be customized based on genre.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.700
Prefer romance movies?

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.729
You’ll probably get this artwork for Goodwill
Hunting.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:51.160
More into comedies than romance?

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.900
You’ll probably get this artwork instead.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.780
There are a lot of different versions of each
image, and you might see certain ones depending

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.060
on what country you’re from, if you’re
using netflix on your phone, computer or TV,

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.760
generally the type of design ascetics that
you like, and many other factors.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.030
In practice its not as simple as just showing
the image that’s most likely to get you

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.030
to click.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.510
There are a lot of things that complicate
the selection process.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.870
To predict what you’ll prefer Netflix is
tracking everything you do one the site, what

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.470
you click on, what you watch, how long you
watch it, and the key thing they’re looking

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.420
for is called a “quality engagement”.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.900
Netflix wants to you to find something you
actually want to watch, so if you click on

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.510
something, and then stop watching it, that
defeats the purpose.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:37.370
Because of this, they try to avoid clickbait
images that misrepresent the piece of media.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.120
Netflix also has to take into consideration
the look of the page as a whole.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.099
A certain image might stand out just because
it’s different from the rest of the artwork

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:46.200
on the page.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.380
For example, images with a large emotional
face generally get the most clicks, but if

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.680
all the artwork is of a large emotional face
an image might stand out and get a click just

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.099
because it’s unique.

00:02:56.099 --> 00:03:00.140
You can learn a lot about what netflix thinks
you like by looking at your home page.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.770
Glancing over mine and my wife’s it’s
clear we like to watch a lot of crime dramas,

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.209
documentaries and historical fiction.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:10.709
Look at on of my brother’s home page and
you’ll see a lot of action adventure, animation,

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:19.440
and fantasy content.Each of these have a unique
look and feel and it’s not just the images

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.460
that netflix is customizing.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.520
Each row is selected and arranged algorithms,
and the contents of each row are chosen to

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.099
help you find content that you’re interested
in watching.

00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:31.989
Even the number or rows shown is personalized
based on your viewing habits.

00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:35.819
Netflix is trying to balance showing you new
things, and changing the layout so that it’s

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:38.900
fresh, with staying consistent enough that
you can find something you saw before.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.990
Change a show’s artwork, and you might re-consider
watching it, change it too often and you might

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.930
have trouble finding it the next time you’re
looking for it.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.660
They’re trying to show people videos like
the stuff they already watch, but they also

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.290
know that everyone wants to branch out and
try something new at some point.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.050
There are thousands of variables, and infinite
combinations, but the result is a unique page

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:52.220
for each user.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.879
An interesting rule of thumb is that the stuff
found in the top left corner of the page is

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:00.959
what Neflix thinks you’re most likely to
watch, and the stuff found in the bottom right

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:03.650
corner, is what netflix thinks you’re least
likely to watch.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.690
And this personalization is everywhere.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.360
Search results are personalized.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.209
Think what shows up in your trending now row
is the same as everyone else?

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:14.360
Nope, those are trending items chosen just
for you.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.450
These three screenshots were taken from different
profiles on the same day at the same time.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.100
They even show you the “continue watching”
row based on whether or not they think you’re

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.620
in the middle of binging something, or if
you’re in the mood for something new.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.150
I wanted to conduct an experiment to see how
easily you could influence this system.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.290
I started up a fresh profile, and decided
to only click on artwork that didn’t have

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.030
people or faces.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.370
This proved to be difficult as almost all
the artwork initially contained a person.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.690
But if I kept choosing only images without
people, would that change?

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:44.690
This kind of personalization in services is
an interesting phenomenon.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.520
It’s easy to imagine a future where almost
every element of a service is personalized

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.560
for you, right down to the interface design,
or the copy used to describe something.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.260
And this might be great thing.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.280
For the most part we all love it when something
we use fits how we use it.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.130
But it’s also easy to imagine a future where
this could go wrong.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:06.330
If Netflix bases their personalization on
certain assumptions, like “if you spend

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.310
more time watching netflix, you’re less
likely to cancel your subscription.”

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.650
There could be some unintended results.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:17.151
What if the personalization get so good that
you start watching too much netflix, and in

00:05:17.151 --> 00:05:22.500
an effort to be more productive you cancel
your subscription to remove the temptation?

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.650
You can see things like this happening already
with Facebook.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:29.310
Facebook has gotten very good at showing you
things, and giving you the notifications that

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.370
keep you coming back to Facebook.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.650
But the more time people spend on Facebook,
the more many people realize they don’t

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.680
want to be spending a lot of time on Facebook.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.630
And often it’s easier to just leave altogether
than it is to try to cut back.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.900
Hopefully companies and personalization AI’s
will realize this, and they’ll start to

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.710
customize things so that you watch enough,
but not too much.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.650
Even if they’re motivated purely by profit,
hopefully Netflix will realize that the best

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:58.450
netflix user isn’t one who watch Netflix
for 6 hours a day, but one that has a healthy

00:05:58.450 --> 00:05:59.810
active lifestyle.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:04.000
Because not watching netflix so much could
ad years to your lifespan effectively allowing

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.789
you to subscribe to netflix for longer.

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:11.639
Back to my experiment, you can see that after
just a few days Netflix began showing me many

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:14.449
more images without people or faces.

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:18.770
The algorithm responded quickly to my fake
preferences, even going against what works

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.270
for the majority.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:24.950
It’s good for us to be aware of how things
are being personalized and customized for

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:25.950
us.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.090
It’s fairly innocent when it’s just Netflix
helping you find a show you’ll like.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:33.639
But what happens when our news is personalized
for what we like, or our search results.

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:38.770
I can already imagine a Black Mirror episode
where Wikipedia is personalized to contain

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:40.389
the facts that you find interesting.

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:50.570
Don’t get me wrong, I love what Netflix
is doing.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:57.050
It’s why there app feel light years ahead
of most of the other stream video apps out

00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:58.050
there.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:03.460
But we can’t be naive to what’s going
on behind the scenes and how it might effects

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.180
us.

